#!/usr/bin/env bash
# gitignore-secret-family-check — for every .gitignore rule that names a SECRET-BEARING file,
# assert that the backup/editor/packaging siblings of that file are ignored too.
#
# THE DEFECT THIS EXISTS FOR. A .gitignore rule covers the NAME, not the FAMILY. `.env` being
# ignored says nothing about `.env~`, `.env.bak`, `.env.save`, `.env.dpkg-old` or `#.env#` —
# each of which holds a byte-identical copy of the same secret and is one `git add -A` from
# being published. Nothing reds when one appears: the file is simply not ignored, so it sits in
# `git status` as an ordinary untracked file among others. Found live on a peer install as a
# 4,339-byte `.env~`, mode 600, that had been in the tree for nine days.
#
# ⛔ WHY A GUARD AND NOT JUST BETTER RULES. The rules were fixed once already, per-rule, and the
# fix's OWN FIRST PASS omitted `*.swp` — leaving `auth.json.swp` stageable. The defect bit the
# person fixing it, inside the fix, and was caught by a manual sweep that will not run again.
# The remaining enumeration is of AFFIXES, and gitignore has no "any backup of this" concept, so
# that floor cannot be removed — only held level with the affixes the real world produces. That
# is this tool's whole job.
#
# THE TWO DERIVATIONS, STATED — neither is a list of filenames, which is the defect one level up.
#
#   1. WHICH RULES ARE SECRET-BEARING is derived from the .gitignore itself, two ways, and a rule
#      qualifying either way is in the population:
#        * RULE-TEXT — the rule's own text carries a secret-ish keyword (`env`, `token`,
#          `secret`, `credential`, `auth`, `.key`, `.pem`, `netrc`, `id_rsa`, …).
#        * COMMENT-BLOCK — the contiguous comment block immediately above the rule carries one.
#          This is what covers a rule whose NAME says nothing (`.kanban-*`, `serviceaccount.json`)
#          but which sits under a heading that does. Blocks are re-opened at every comment run,
#          so a later non-secret heading does not inherit an earlier secret one.
#      A rule added to a repo tomorrow is therefore classified by the same predicate, with no
#      edit here — which is the property a hand list of rules cannot have.
#
#   2. WHICH AFFIXES A FAMILY HAS is a DECLARED enumeration (`SUFFIX_AFFIXES`/`WRAP_AFFIXES`
#      below), because there is nothing to derive it from. ⚑ EVERY ROW CARRIES ITS PROVENANCE,
#      and the tags are not decoration — they are the honest limit of this table:
#        measured    — observed directly on this box (the producer was run and its answer read).
#        documented  — read out of that producer's own man page, present on this box.
#        vim-doc     — vim's documented swap/undo naming; `directory` was measured, the NAMES were not.
#        partly-doc  — the SHAPE is documented, the exact spelling was not verified.
#        reported    — taken from the incident this tool exists for.
#        convention  — a real habit, but NOT sourced. Recall, written down as recall.
#        unsourced   — the producer is NOT INSTALLED HERE and its documentation was NOT read.
#      The `convention` and `unsourced` rows are the weak ones and are labelled so nobody cites
#      this table as uniformly evidence-backed. They cost nothing to keep — a spurious affix
#      only ever demands MORE coverage — but they must not be quoted as sourced facts.
#      That table is this tool's floor and its limit, and it is a LOWER BOUND: see the scope
#      statement below.
#
# WHAT IT ACTUALLY ASSERTS. For each secret-bearing rule it instantiates one concrete SAMPLE
# path from the glob (`*` and `?` → `x`, `**/` dropped, leading `/` stripped), checks that the
# sample is itself ignored BY THAT RULE — the positive control, without which a broken
# instantiation would produce a sample nothing matches and every later answer would be about the
# wrong path — and then asks `git check-ignore` whether each affixed sibling of that sample is
# ignored. NO FILE IS EVER CREATED IN THE REPOSITORY: `check-ignore` answers about pathnames,
# not about files on disk, so this tool plants no throwaway `.env~` to test with — which matters,
# because a guard that creates the very file it is checking for is one crash away from leaving it
# behind. It writes no temporary file either; the one NUL stream it reads is a pipe.
#
# ⚑ `--no-index` IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT A DETAIL. Measured: `git check-ignore` suppresses paths
# that are in the INDEX and answers rc 1 for them — "not ignored" — even when a rule plainly
# matches. Two consequences, and they point the same way: the question here is about the RULE,
# which the index has no bearing on; and without `--no-index` a TRACKED `.env.example` would be
# reported as an uncovered family member, which is a false finding this tool must never emit.
#
# ⛔⛔ SCOPE — WHAT A GREEN RUN DOES AND DOES NOT SAY. It says: the rules IN THIS REPO, AT THE REF
# THIS RUN READ, cover the declared affix table. It does NOT say:
#   * that any INSTALL is covered. An install pinned to an older tag runs the OLD .gitignore, and
#     this tool cannot see it. That question has to be asked per install, by that install — two
#     different questions, and only this one is greppable from CI.
#   * that the family is closed. The affix table is a LOWER BOUND over an unbounded population:
#     nano writes `.save.1`, `.save.2`, … without limit, `cp .env .env.for-alice` invents a
#     suffix on the spot, and no enumeration reaches either. A rule spelled with a trailing `*`
#     (`.env*`) covers its whole suffix family unconditionally and is the only construction that
#     does; where that does not over-widen, prefer it to relying on this table.
#   * anything about a rule the predicate does not classify as secret-bearing — a rule whose text
#     carries no keyword AND whose comment block carries none is invisible here, silently. That
#     is the one blind spot with no red: over-collection errs RED (a harmless extra demand),
#     under-collection errs GREEN.
#   * anything about files already TRACKED. .gitignore has no effect on them, so neither has this
#     tool; a secret already committed is a different problem with a different remedy.
#
# Usage:
#   gitignore-secret-family-check [--repo PATH] [--allow-empty]
#
#   --repo         the repository to check (default: the current directory)
#   --allow-empty  permit a run that derives ZERO secret-bearing rules to exit 0.
#                  Without it that state is a REFUSAL (rc 2), deliberately: an empty population
#                  satisfies every assertion made over it, so "no findings" over nothing measured
#                  is indistinguishable from a clean repo. A repo that genuinely ignores no
#                  secret-bearing file says so with this flag, on the record.
#
# Exit codes:
#   0  ran, and every derived family member is ignored
#   1  at least one family member is NOT ignored, or a rule's sample could not be resolved
#   2  usage error, or a refusal (not a git repo, no .gitignore, empty population without
#      --allow-empty) — every unmeasurable state fails CLOSED
set -euo pipefail
# ⛔ FIRST STATEMENT AFTER `set`, AND THE POSITION IS LOAD-BEARING — every write in this file is
# below it, including the unknown-option refusal in the arg loop, which a reader that has stopped
# reading turns into a signal death if the trap is not yet in force.
trap '' PIPE

# --- the write rule (card#6911) ---------------------------------------------
# ⛔ THIS TOOL'S WHOLE PRODUCT IS A VERDICT — an rc, plus the FINDINGS block explaining it — and
# the findings go to STDOUT, in front of the verdict-carrying `exit`. A consumer that stops
# reading (`| head`, a quit pager, a `$(…)` whose caller already died) would destroy BOTH, and
# the invocation would look like it worked: a security gate reporting nothing, at a signal rc
# that no caller reads as "uncovered secret family". That is strictly worse than no gate.
#
# The same TWO properties the runtime-check fix uses, and both are needed:
#   1. `trap '' PIPE` above — a closed reader makes a write RETURN EPIPE instead of killing the
#      process. Alone it is NOT enough: errexit then takes the write's own non-zero rc, which
#      here would report rc 1 — "uncovered family members" — for a repo that is in fact clean.
#      A wrong verdict, not a crash.
#   2. ONE write per stream, of an ALREADY-BUILT string, whose failure is tolerated: by the time
#      it is attempted there is nothing left to lose.
# ⚠ THE TOLERANCE IS UNTARGETED — `|| true` swallows every write error, not only EPIPE, because
# bash cannot tell them apart at `printf`'s return. So "nothing left to lose" is true of the
# VERDICT, which rides the exit code, and FALSE of the report text: with stdout on a full
# filesystem this tool exits with the right rc having printed nothing. Accepted on the record.
_put()     { [ -z "$1" ] || printf '%s' "$1" 2>/dev/null || true; }
_put_err() { [ -z "$1" ] || printf '%s' "$1" >&2 2>/dev/null || true; }

_gsf_die() { _put_err "gitignore-secret-family-check: $1"$'\n'; exit "${2:-2}"; }

_gsf_help() {
    local h
    h="$(awk 'NR>1 { if (substr($0,1,1) == "#") { sub(/^# ?/, ""); print } else exit }' "$0")"
    _put "$h"$'\n'
}

REPO="."
ALLOW_EMPTY=0
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
        -h|--help) _gsf_help; exit 0 ;;
        --repo) [ -n "${2:-}" ] || _gsf_die "--repo requires a non-empty value"; REPO="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --allow-empty) ALLOW_EMPTY=1; shift ;;
        -*) _gsf_die "unknown option '$1' (try --help)" ;;
        *)  _gsf_die "unexpected argument '$1' (try --help)" ;;
    esac
done

[ -d "$REPO" ] || _gsf_die "not a directory: $REPO"
cd "$REPO" || _gsf_die "cannot enter: $REPO"
git rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree >/dev/null 2>&1 || _gsf_die "not inside a git work tree: $REPO"

# ── DERIVATION 1: what makes a rule secret-bearing ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Case-insensitive, matched against the rule's own text and against its comment block. Kept as
# KEYWORDS rather than filenames on purpose: a keyword set is a far more stable population than
# the set of files that hold secrets, and a new secret file inherits classification for free.
KEYWORDS='env|secret|token|credential|password|passwd|auth|apikey|api[-_]key|\.key|keystore|keyring|\.pem|\.p12|\.pfx|\.jks|netrc|npmrc|pypirc|pgpass|htpasswd|id_rsa|id_ed25519|id_dsa|id_ecdsa'

# ── DERIVATION 2: the affix table ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SUFFIX_AFFIXES are appended to the sample. Column 2 is the PROVENANCE TAG; the tags and what
# each one licenses you to claim are defined ONCE, in DERIVATION 2 of the header above — not
# restated here. (An earlier draft restated it as "the producer's own docs or a direct
# measurement, never recall", which the `convention` and `unsourced` rows make false.)
SUFFIX_AFFIXES='
~                 measured      vim backupext=~; emacs make-backup-file-name(/tmp/.env)=/tmp/.env~; cp(1) default
.~1~              documented    cp(1) --backup=numbered, man 1 cp
.bak              convention    widespread `cp x x.bak`; NOT sourced on this box
.bak-20260717     reported      the ad-hoc dated copy in the incident this tool came from
.bak.20260717     convention    the dotted spelling of the same habit
.orig             documented    patch(1) SIMPLE_BACKUP_SUFFIX default; git-mergetool(1) keepBackup
.rej              documented    patch(1) reject file
.save             documented    nano(1) emergency save
.save.1           documented    nano(1) uniquifies a colliding emergency save with .save.N
.swp              vim-doc       vim swapfile; `directory` measured as `.` here, so it lands beside the file
.swo              vim-doc       vim second swapfile
.swn              vim-doc       vim third swapfile
.un~              vim-doc       vim persistent undo (already covered by the ~ row; kept for the assertion)
.old              convention    rotation habit; NOT sourced
.tmp              convention    NOT sourced
.copy             convention    duplicate-file habit; NOT sourced, and GUI managers actually spell it
                                `name (copy)` — this row does not cover that spelling
.dpkg-old         documented    dpkg(1) conffile handling
.dpkg-dist        documented    dpkg(1) conffile handling
.dpkg-new         documented    dpkg(1) conffile handling
.rpmsave          unsourced     rpm conffile handling — rpm is NOT installed here, so this was NOT read
.rpmnew           unsourced     rpm conffile handling — NOT read on this box
.ucf-old          unsourced     ucf conffile handling — ucf is NOT installed here, so this was NOT read
.ucf-dist         unsourced     ucf conffile handling — NOT read on this box
.BACKUP.12345     partly-doc    git-mergetool(1) documents BASE/LOCAL/REMOTE temporaries; the exact
                                `<name>.BACKUP.<pid>` spelling was NOT verified here
.LOCAL.12345      partly-doc    as above
.REMOTE.12345     partly-doc    as above
.BASE.12345       partly-doc    as above
'
# WRAP_AFFIXES wrap the BASENAME rather than appending to it — a shape no `*.suffix` rule can
# ever reach, which is why they are a separate list and not more rows above. `#<name>#` is
# emacs's auto-save file, measured (`make-auto-save-file-name` on `/tmp/.env` → `/tmp/#.env#`);
# `.#<name>` is its lock file.
WRAP_AFFIXES='#%s# .#%s'

# AFFIXES — the affix column of SUFFIX_AFFIXES, parsed ONCE into an array that both call sites
# below read. A row starts at column 0 with `.` or `~`; the provenance notes wrap onto INDENTED
# continuation lines, which must not become members.
#
# ⚑ WHICH HALF DOES THE WORK, stated exactly, because the two are easy to confuse: the
# EXTRACTION does. `${line%%[[:space:]]*}` cuts at the FIRST whitespace, and a continuation line
# begins with whitespace, so it yields the empty string and is skipped. The earlier reader was
# `read -r a _`, which strips leading whitespace BEFORE splitting — so a continuation line handed
# back its first word and `\`name` entered the population as an affix, measured, reported as an
# uncovered family member of every secret rule. Prose entering the population is this tool's own
# defect one level up.
# The `[.~]*` case is the ROW RULE written down, not a second guard against that: it makes a
# malformed table (a row that does not start at column 0 with `.` or `~`) drop out visibly here
# rather than depend on the extraction happening to empty it.
#
# ⛔ AN ARRAY, NOT A FUNCTION STREAMED THROUGH A PIPE, and that is not a style choice.
# `_gsf_is_affix_rule` returns from INSIDE its loop on the first match, so a
# `while … done < <(_emit_affixes)` reader leaves early and closes the pipe while the writer is
# still writing. Under the `trap '' PIPE` above that is not a silent death — the writer's printf
# returns EPIPE and bash prints `write error: Broken pipe` onto this tool's own stderr, which is
# the surface its verdict is read from. An array has no reader and no writer to desynchronise.
AFFIXES=()
while IFS= read -r _line; do
    case "$_line" in
        [.~]*) AFFIXES+=("${_line%%[[:space:]]*}") ;;
    esac
done <<< "$SUFFIX_AFFIXES"

# ── the scan ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
gitignores="$(command find . -name .gitignore -not -path './.git/*' -type f | sed 's|^\./||' | LC_ALL=C sort)"
[ -n "$gitignores" ] || _gsf_die "no .gitignore found under $REPO — nothing to check"

# _gsf_sample <rule> — one concrete path the rule matches, or "" if the rule names no file.
#
# Walked CHARACTER BY CHARACTER rather than done with substitutions, because gitignore's
# BACKSLASH ESCAPE makes the two orders disagree. `\#*#` is a rule matching `#<anything>#` —
# the backslash is syntax, not a character in the name — so a naive strip produces the sample
# `\#x#`, which nothing matches, and the rule reads as UNRESOLVED. Escapes must therefore be
# resolved in the SAME pass as the wildcards, never before them (`\*` is a literal asterisk and
# must not become a wildcard) and never after (the leading `\` would survive into the sample).
_gsf_sample() {
    printf '%s' "$1" | awk '{
        s = $0
        sub(/^\//, "", s)
        gsub(/\*\*\//, "", s)
        out = ""
        for (i = 1; i <= length(s); i++) {
            c = substr(s, i, 1)
            if (c == "\\") { i++; out = out substr(s, i, 1) }
            else if (c == "*" || c == "?") { out = out "x" }
            else if (c == "[") { while (i <= length(s) && substr(s, i, 1) != "]") i++; out = out "x" }
            else out = out c
        }
        printf "%s", out
    }'
}

# _gsf_is_affix_rule <sample> — is this rule ITSELF a backup-family rule (`*~`, `*.bak`, `#*#`)?
# Such a rule is the remedy, not a secret-bearing file, and asking for the backup-of-a-backup
# family is noise. Excluded by SHAPE, and the exclusion is printed on every run.
_gsf_is_affix_rule() {
    local s="$1" a
    case "$s" in '#'*'#'|'.#'*) return 0 ;; esac
    for a in "${AFFIXES[@]}"; do
        case "$s" in *"$a") return 0 ;; esac
    done
    return 1
}

n_files=0; n_rules=0; n_secret=0; n_assert=0; n_uncovered=0; n_unresolved=0
skipped=""
findings=""

for ign in $gitignores; do
    n_files=$((n_files + 1))
    ln=0; blk=0; prev=x
    while IFS= read -r rule || [ -n "$rule" ]; do
        ln=$((ln + 1))
        rule="${rule%$'\r'}"
        case "$rule" in
            '') prev=x; blk=0; continue ;;
            '#'*)
                [ "$prev" = c ] || blk=0
                prev=c
                printf '%s' "$rule" | command grep -qiE "$KEYWORDS" && blk=1
                continue ;;
        esac
        prev=r
        n_rules=$((n_rules + 1))
        # A negation UN-ignores; it names no secret to protect a family of.
        case "$rule" in '!'*) continue ;; esac

        why=""
        if printf '%s' "$rule" | command grep -qiE "$KEYWORDS"; then why="rule-text"
        elif [ "$blk" = 1 ]; then why="comment-block"
        else continue
        fi

        # A directory rule names no file, so it has no backup sibling. Dispositioned, not dropped.
        case "$rule" in */) skipped="$skipped\n  SKIP dir-rule      $ign:$ln  $rule"; continue ;; esac

        sample="$(_gsf_sample "$rule")"
        [ -n "$sample" ] || { skipped="$skipped\n  SKIP no-sample     $ign:$ln  $rule"; continue; }
        if _gsf_is_affix_rule "$sample"; then
            skipped="$skipped\n  SKIP affix-rule    $ign:$ln  $rule  (this rule IS backup-family coverage)"
            continue
        fi

        n_secret=$((n_secret + 1))

        # Build the candidate set: the sample itself (the positive control) then every affix.
        dir=""; base="$sample"
        case "$sample" in */*) dir="${sample%/*}/"; base="${sample##*/}" ;; esac
        cands=("$sample")
        for a in "${AFFIXES[@]}"; do
            cands+=("$sample$a")
        done
        for w in $WRAP_AFFIXES; do
            cands+=("$dir${w//%s/$base}")
        done

        # ONE check-ignore call for the whole set. `-n` makes it emit a record for every input,
        # matching or not, so a path is never silently absent from the answer. The NUL stream is
        # read through a process-substitution REDIRECT, never `$(...)`: command substitution
        # discards NUL bytes, which would silently collapse the field framing.
        ctl_src=""; ctl_seen=0; unc=""
        while IFS= read -r -d '' src && IFS= read -r -d '' _l && IFS= read -r -d '' _p \
              && IFS= read -r -d '' path; do
            if [ "$path" = "$sample" ]; then ctl_src="$src"; ctl_seen=1; continue; fi
            n_assert=$((n_assert + 1))
            [ -n "$src" ] || unc="$unc ${path#"$sample"}"
        done < <(printf '%s\0' "${cands[@]}" | git check-ignore -v -n -z --no-index --stdin || true)

        # POSITIVE CONTROL. The sample must be ignored, or every answer above is about a path
        # this rule does not even match — an UNRESOLVED member no hand list can contain, rather
        # than a silently shrunken population.
        if [ "$ctl_seen" -eq 0 ] || [ -z "$ctl_src" ]; then
            n_unresolved=$((n_unresolved + 1))
            findings="$findings\n  UNRESOLVED $ign:$ln  $rule\n             sample '$sample' is not ignored by anything — the instantiation, not the repo, is wrong"
            continue
        fi

        if [ -n "$unc" ]; then
            n_uncovered=$((n_uncovered + 1))
            findings="$findings\n  UNCOVERED  $ign:$ln  $rule   [$why]\n             sample: $sample\n             family members NOT ignored:$unc"
        fi
    done < "$ign"
done

# ── the report ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ONE stdout write, of a string built in full first — property 2 of the write rule above. The
# report is ACCUMULATED here and emitted at each exit, never printed as it is computed: a
# printf-as-you-go report puts N chances to lose the verdict in front of the `exit` that carries
# it, which is the whole defect.
R="gitignore-secret-family-check — $(git rev-parse --short HEAD 2>/dev/null || echo '(no HEAD)')"$'\n'
R="$R$(printf '  scanned      %d .gitignore file(s), %d rule(s)' "$n_files" "$n_rules")"$'\n'
R="$R$(printf '  secret-bearing rules derived: %d' "$n_secret")"$'\n'
R="$R$(printf '  family assertions made:       %d' "$n_assert")"$'\n'
[ -n "$skipped" ] && R="$R$(printf '  dispositioned (not asserted):%b' "$skipped")"$'\n'

# The population's own positive control, before any verdict is reported.
if [ "$n_secret" -eq 0 ]; then
    if [ "$ALLOW_EMPTY" -eq 1 ]; then
        _put "$R  ZERO secret-bearing rules derived; --allow-empty was given, so this is an ANSWER."$'\n'
        exit 0
    fi
    _put "$R"
    _gsf_die "derived ZERO secret-bearing rules from $n_rules rule(s) in $n_files file(s). Nothing was measured, so \"no findings\" would mean nothing. Either this repo ignores no secret-bearing file (pass --allow-empty to say so on the record) or the predicate is broken." 2
fi
if [ "$n_assert" -eq 0 ]; then
    _put "$R"
    _gsf_die "derived $n_secret secret-bearing rule(s) but made ZERO assertions — the affix table is empty or unreadable. Refusing to report clean." 2
fi

if [ -n "$findings" ]; then
    R="$R"$'\n'"$(printf 'FINDINGS:%b' "$findings")"$'\n'
    R="$R"$'\n'"REMEDY — do NOT add one rule per member. Either give the rule a trailing '*' so it covers
its whole suffix family unconditionally (\`.env\` -> \`.env*\`), which is the only complete fix,
or extend the ONE universal backup-affix block that covers every file in the repo. A per-rule
enumeration is what put this defect here: it is permanently one editor behind, and the next
secret file added will not inherit it.

SCOPE OF THIS VERDICT: the rules in THIS repository at THIS ref. It says nothing about which ref
any install actually runs — an install pinned to an older tag runs the old rules and must be
asked separately — and the affix table is a LOWER BOUND on an unbounded family."$'\n'
    _put "$R"
    exit 1
fi

R="$R$(printf '  all %d family member(s) of %d secret-bearing rule(s) are ignored.' "$n_assert" "$n_secret")"$'\n'
R="$R  SCOPE: the rules in THIS repo at THIS ref, against a LOWER-BOUND affix table. Not a"$'\n'
R="$R         statement about any deployed install, nor about files already tracked."$'\n'
_put "$R"
exit 0
